Feature visualization
=====================

PyTorch code for https://henrikaalto.fi/blog/feature-visualization.
Each recipe optimizes an image against a frozen Inception v1.

Install uv, then run:

    uv run --locked python -m scripts.reproduce all

Outputs are individual PNGs under output/<method>/. The cover command
writes its ten tiles in reading order. Images can vary across devices.

Run a single recipe or a single channel:

    uv run --locked python -m scripts.reproduce fourier
    uv run --locked python -m scripts.reproduce maco --target mixed4d:485
    uv run --locked python -m scripts.reproduce all --steps 4 --output output/smoke

The cloud DeepDream recipe is available separately:

    uv run --locked python -m scripts.reproduce dream

Run with --help for the method list and device options. The default uses
CUDA or Metal when available and otherwise uses CPU. The full run can
take a while. --steps shortens it for a quick check.

scripts/       Entry point and the blog's target channels
recipes/       Pixel ascent, Fourier methods, CPPN, and DeepDream
shared/        Inception v1, the optimization loop, and image transforms
assets/        Frozen weights, magnitude prior, and cloud input
output/        Generated PNGs

The blog's MACO panels use fixed ImageNet magnitudes and the MACO
decoder with the 2017 views and Adam schedule. The centered panels use
a Gaussian spatial objective and an RGB prior.

The Inception weights were extracted from the frozen Lucid graph:
https://storage.googleapis.com/modelzoo/vision/other_models/InceptionV1.pb

The fixed magnitude prior is from Xplique. Its source, checksum, and
license are in assets/maco_imagenet.json.

The cropped cloud photo is by Rosendahl (public domain):
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clouds_in_blue_sky.jpg

See LICENSES/ for the upstream notices.
